#!/bin/bash
# Refuse a fold that says nothing about what changed.
#
# staging's history is heading toward release notes generated per release, so
# every commit on it has to stand alone as a changelog line. A fold of a feature
# branch is not exempt: `git merge --squash` then ONE commit describing the
# CHANGE is the model, and `git merge` without --squash takes git's default
# subject, which names the branch and says nothing.
#
# 100 of those accumulated on staging before anyone counted, 25 of them in a
# single day. The branch name and the fact that it was a fold are noise.
#
# A merge of a REMOTE tracking branch is a different thing (syncing after a bot
# push) and is allowed through: this only refuses local topic branches.
set -euo pipefail

subject=$(head -1 "$1")

case "$subject" in
  "Merge branch 'fleet/"*|"Merge branch 'worktree-"*|"Merge branch 'agent-"*)
    cat >&2 <<MSG
commit-msg: refusing a fold that describes the fold instead of the change.

  $subject

staging history becomes release notes, so this needs to read as a changelog
line for the feature. Use:

  git merge --squash <branch>
  git commit          # subject: what CHANGED, imperative, no branch name

If you genuinely want a merge commit here, write its subject by hand.
MSG
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

# ── Refuse a body that is not a changelog line ────────────────────────────────
#
# THE STYLE, IN FULL. Duplicated here on purpose rather than linked: a hook that
# points at a document is a hook whose rule nobody reads at the moment it fires.
#
# INTENT: staging's history becomes release notes, generated per release. Every
# commit on it therefore has to stand alone as ONE CHANGELOG LINE, read by
# someone who was not here, has no memory of the session that produced it, and
# wants to know what changed. That single purpose is what every rule below comes
# from.
#
#   1. The SUBJECT carries the change. Imperative, specific, and about what
#      changed rather than what was done to the repository. `feat(x):`/`fix(x):`
#      prefixes are fine and are what the release tooling reads.
#   2. DEFAULT TO NO BODY. On a changelog commit a body is usually superfluous.
#      If the subject cannot carry it, the subject is wrong. Where something
#      genuinely must be said, say it flatly in ONE line.
#   3. NO HISTORY-TELLING. Why the repo was in an odd state, what was tried
#      first, which agent did what, how long it took, what the measurement was:
#      all noise in a changelog. Cut it.
#   4. NO THIRD-PERSON NARRATION. Commits are authored as the repo author.
#      Never "the user", "the operator", "as requested", "user-flagged".
#   5. NO AI ATTRIBUTION of any kind, including a Co-Authored-By trailer.
#   6. A FOLD DESCRIBES THE FEATURE, never the fold. `git merge --squash`, then
#      one commit that reads like the changelog entry for what landed.
#
# Long, verbose, working-scaffolding commits are fine on a FEATURE BRANCH; they
# are discarded at the squash-fold. This hook guards what reaches staging.
#
# Footers are exempt (BREAKING CHANGE:, Refs:, Fixes:) because the release
# tooling reads them. A git-generated revert body is exempt for the same reason
# it exists.

# A co-author trailer is refused whoever it names. It arrives as a well-formed
# trailer, so the body rule below counts it as an exempt footer and lets it
# through; that was found by testing the hook rather than by reading it.
#
# Not an anti-assistant rule, though an assistant trailer is banned outright and
# must never be written: attribution of any kind is not a changelog line. Who
# wrote a change belongs in the commit's author field, which git already has.
if sed -e '/^#/d' "$1" | grep -qiE '^(co-authored-by|generated-with|assisted-by):'; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'
commit-msg: refusing an attribution trailer.

Attribution is not a changelog line, and git already records who authored the
commit. End the message at the subject.
MSG
  exit 1
fi

body=$(sed -e '/^#/d' "$1" | tail -n +2)
prose=$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*$' | grep -vE '^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*|BREAKING CHANGE): ' || true)
prose_lines=$(printf '%s' "$prose" | grep -c . || true)

case "$subject" in
  "Revert "*) prose_lines=0 ;;
esac

if [ "${prose_lines:-0}" -gt 1 ]; then
  cat >&2 <<MSG
commit-msg: refusing a commit body of $prose_lines lines.

staging history becomes release notes, so this commit has to stand alone as ONE
CHANGELOG LINE for someone who was not here. A body is usually superfluous: if
the subject cannot carry the change, the subject is wrong.

  $subject

Cut the body, or reduce it to a single flat line. In particular cut:
  - history-telling (what was tried first, why the repo was odd, measurements)
  - third-person narration ("the user", "the operator", "as requested")
  - any AI attribution, including a Co-Authored-By trailer

Verbose working commits belong on a feature branch and are discarded at the
squash-fold. Footers (BREAKING CHANGE:, Refs:, Fixes:) are exempt and do not
count toward this.
MSG
  exit 1
fi

# The repo's stated style is Conventional Commits, and until 2026-09-06 nothing
# here checked for a type at all: "TestFlight has left: remove the in-repo copy"
# passed, and six such subjects reached staging. The scope pattern allows
# SPACES, because `test(space centre):` and `fix(kerbalism uplink):` are already
# in use and a stricter pattern would reject the history it is meant to protect.
case "$subject" in
  "Revert "*|"Merge "*) ;;
  *)
    if ! printf '%s' "$subject" | grep -qE '^(feat|fix|docs|style|refactor|perf|test|build|ci|chore|revert)(\([^)]+\))?!?: .'; then
      cat >&2 <<MSG
commit-msg: refusing a subject with no Conventional Commits type.

  $subject

staging history becomes release notes, and the type is what sorts a change into
them. Start with one of:
  feat fix docs style refactor perf test build ci chore revert
optionally scoped, e.g. fix(rail): or test(space centre):, then ": " and the
change in the imperative.
MSG
      exit 1
    fi
    ;;
esac
